---
import AgentScene from "./AgentScene.astro";
---

<div class="kd-home kd-agents">
  <header class="kd-start-hero kd-agents-hero">
    <p class="kd-home-eyebrow">Official agent skill</p>
    <h1 id="_top">Diagrams your agent can defend.</h1>
    <p class="kd-start-hero__lede">
      Generic AI fills gaps with invented boxes. The Kekonic Diagrams skill makes Cursor, Claude
      Code, Codex, and other coding agents ground every node in the repository, choose the right
      view, and verify the file with the same CLI you run in CI.
    </p>
    <div class="kd-start-install" aria-label="Install the Kekonic Diagrams agent skill">
      <span>$</span>
      <code>npx skills add kekonic/diagrams</code>
    </div>
    <p class="kd-agents-hosts">Cursor · Claude Code · Codex · any Skills CLI host</p>
    <div class="kd-start-actions">
      <a class="kd-cta kd-cta--primary" href="/start/quick-start/">Get started</a>
      <a class="kd-cta kd-cta--ghost" href="/studio/">Open Studio</a>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="kd-start-section kd-agents-proof" aria-labelledby="kd-agents-proof-title">
    <div class="kd-start-section__head">
      <h2 id="kd-agents-proof-title">The deliverable is a model, not a one-shot image.</h2>
      <p>
        Ask for one reader question. The skill inspects the repo, records what it actually found,
        flags guesses, writes `.kdiagram` source, and checks the result before it comes back to you.
      </p>
    </div>
    <AgentScene />
  </section>

  <section class="kd-start-section" aria-labelledby="kd-agents-why-title">
    <div class="kd-start-section__head">
      <h2 id="kd-agents-why-title">Why this is a must-have, not a novelty.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="kd-start-pillars">
      <article>
        <span class="kd-start-pillars__number">01</span>
        <h3>Grounded in the repo</h3>
        <p>
          The skill forbids inventing a service, queue, owner, protocol, or database to make the
          picture feel complete. Missing facts stay in the returned notes.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="kd-start-pillars__number">02</span>
        <h3>Checked like code</h3>
        <p>
          Agents validate, format, render, and analyze with the Kekonic Diagrams CLI. They get
          structured diagnostics and quality warnings, not a hunch about whether the SVG looks
          right.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="kd-start-pillars__number">03</span>
        <h3>Reviewed in Git</h3>
        <p>
          The file is ordinary text beside the system it explains. A pull request can reject an
          invented box the same way it rejects an invented API.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="kd-start-section" aria-labelledby="kd-agents-skill-title">
    <div class="kd-start-section__head">
      <h2 id="kd-agents-skill-title">What the skill actually teaches.</h2>
      <p>
        <code>design-kekonic-diagrams</code> is an expert workflow, not a grammar dump. Detailed
        pattern and repair references load only when the task needs them.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ol class="kd-agents-steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Name the reader and the question.</strong>
        One decision per diagram. “Show checkout” is too broad; “how a successful order reaches
        payment authorization” is a job.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Separate facts from inference.</strong>
        What you said, what the repo shows, and what the agent assumed stay distinct.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Choose the view before the boxes.</strong>
        Architecture, events, workflow, ERD, sequence, or state — split the work when one picture
        is being asked to explain everything.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Keep <code>.kdiagram</code> source as the source of truth.</strong>
        Presentation knobs stay in the DSL. No hidden editor state.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Verify with deterministic tools.</strong>
        <code>kdiagrams check</code>, <code>format</code>, <code>render</code>, and
        <code>analyze</code> close the loop.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Return the whole packet.</strong>
        Source, artifact, assumptions, intentional omissions, and unresolved questions together.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="kd-start-section kd-agents-prompt" aria-labelledby="kd-agents-prompt-title">
    <div class="kd-start-section__head">
      <h2 id="kd-agents-prompt-title">A prompt that uses the skill well.</h2>
      <p>
        Give the agent a precise reader question and say which repository facts it may inspect.
        Review the assumptions as carefully as the diagram.
      </p>
    </div>
    <blockquote>
      Use the Kekonic Diagrams skill to show a new engineer how checkout moves from the web app
      through payment authorization. Read the relevant code and configuration, distinguish observed
      facts from assumptions, and return the <code>.kdiagram</code> source plus a portable SVG.
    </blockquote>
  </section>

  <section class="kd-start-section kd-start-delivery" aria-labelledby="kd-agents-install-title">
    <div class="kd-start-section__head">
      <h2 id="kd-agents-install-title">Install it where your agent already works.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="kd-start-delivery__grid">
      <div>
        <span>Skills CLI</span>
        <strong>Host-neutral skill</strong>
        <p>
          <code>npx skills add kekonic/diagrams</code>
        </p>
        <p>
          From git:
          <code>npx skills add https://github.com/kekonic/diagrams/tree/main/packages/diagrams-agents</code>
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span>npm</span>
        <strong>@kekonic/diagrams-agents</strong>
        <p>
          The same files ship as a package for dependency-managed agent environments.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span>CLI</span>
        <strong>Deterministic tools</strong>
        <p>
          <code>kdiagrams check</code>, <code>render</code>, <code>analyze</code>, and
          <code>capabilities</code> are the feedback loop.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span>Next</span>
        <strong>Same operations, more hosts</strong>
        <p>
          Machine-readable capabilities, quality diagnostics, semantic comparison, and MCP will
          expose the same public pipeline without duplicating it per agent host.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="kd-start-close" aria-labelledby="kd-agents-close-title">
    <h2 id="kd-agents-close-title">Give your agent the skill. Keep the diagram in Git.</h2>
    <div class="kd-start-actions">
      <a class="kd-cta kd-cta--primary" href="/start/quick-start/">Open the quickstart</a>
      <a class="kd-cta kd-cta--ghost" href="/gallery/">Browse source examples</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>
